## 2. Covariant constitutive structure of spacetime

### 2.1 Maxwell theory without a metric-first formulation

Let \(M\) be an oriented four-dimensional differentiable manifold. In differential-form notation Maxwell's equations may be written

\[
dF=0,
\qquad
dH=J,
\tag{2}
\]

where \(F\) is the electromagnetic field-strength two-form, \(H\) is the excitation two-form and \(J\) is the electric current three-form.

Equations (2) do not themselves determine the relationship between \(F\) and \(H\). Vacuum electrodynamics introduces the constitutive law

\[
H=\lambda_0\,\star_g F,
\tag{3}
\]

where \(\lambda_0\) is the vacuum admittance and

\[
\star_g:\Lambda^2T^\ast M
\rightarrow
\Lambda^2T^\ast M
\tag{4}
\]

is the Hodge operator generated by the spacetime metric.

In components this may equivalently be written

\[
H^{\mu\nu}
=
\frac{\lambda_0}{2}
\chi^{\mu\nu\rho\sigma}[g]F_{\rho\sigma},
\tag{5}
\]

where, up to the choice of tensor-density convention,

\[
\chi^{\mu\nu\rho\sigma}[g]
=
\sqrt{-g}
\left(
g^{\mu\rho}g^{\nu\sigma}
-
g^{\mu\sigma}g^{\nu\rho}
\right).
\tag{6}
\]

The metric therefore enters electromagnetism precisely through a constitutive relation. Premetric analyses show conversely that symmetry and closure conditions on an appropriate local linear constitutive tensor determine a conformal Lorentzian metric.

This observation motivates TPV but is not itself sufficient to reconstruct all of GR.

### 2.2 The conformal-scale problem

In four spacetime dimensions, the Hodge operator on two-forms is invariant under

\[
g_{\mu\nu}\rightarrow
\Omega^2(x)g_{\mu\nu}.
\tag{7}
\]

Consequently,

\[
\star_{\Omega^2 g}\big|_{\Lambda^2}
=
\star_g\big|_{\Lambda^2}.
\tag{8}
\]

The electromagnetic constitutive tensor therefore determines the **conformal class**

\[
[g]=
\{\Omega^2g\},
\tag{9}
\]

but not the absolute conformal factor.

This is not a minor technicality. A theory claiming exact equivalence to GR cannot identify the full metric with the electromagnetic constitutive tensor alone.

The minimal TPV state must therefore contain one additional geometric quantity carrying the conformal scale. The natural choice is the metric volume four-form,

\[
\boldsymbol{\epsilon}_g
=
\sqrt{-g}\,
dx^0\wedge dx^1\wedge dx^2\wedge dx^3 .
\tag{10}
\]

We define the complete TPV constitutive state as

\[
\boxed{
\mathcal C[g]
\equiv
\left(
\star_g\big|_{\Lambda^2},
\boldsymbol{\epsilon}_g
\right).
}
\tag{11}
\]

The first component determines causal/conformal geometry; the second fixes its scale.

