## 4. TPV action

The Einstein-Hilbert-matter action is

\[
S_{\rm GR}[g,\Psi]
=
\frac{1}{2\kappa}
\int_M
\boldsymbol{\epsilon}_g
\left(R[g]-2\Lambda\right)
+
S_m[g,\Psi].
\tag{23}
\]

Here \(\Psi\) denotes the complete collection of matter fields.

The baseline TPV action is defined simply by composition with the inverse constitutive map:

\[
\boxed{
S_{\rm TPV}[\mathcal C,\Psi]
=
S_{\rm GR}[g[\mathcal C],\Psi].
}
\tag{24}
\]

Equation (24) is not an approximation, low-energy expansion or matching prescription. It is a change of variables on the gravitational configuration space.

Let \(E^{\mu\nu}\) denote the metric Euler-Lagrange derivative,

\[
E^{\mu\nu}
=
\frac{2}{\sqrt{-g}}
\frac{\delta S_{\rm GR}}{\delta g_{\mu\nu}}.
\tag{25}
\]

An admissible TPV variation satisfies

\[
\delta g_{\mu\nu}
=
\frac{\delta g_{\mu\nu}}
{\delta \mathcal C^A}
\delta\mathcal C^A ,
\tag{26}
\]

where \(A\) labels independent tangent directions on
\(\mathscr C_{\rm TPV}\).

Then

\[
\delta S_{\rm TPV}
=
\frac12
\int d^4x\sqrt{-g}\,
E^{\mu\nu}
\frac{\delta g_{\mu\nu}}
{\delta\mathcal C^A}
\delta\mathcal C^A
+
\frac{\delta S}{\delta\Psi}\delta\Psi.
\tag{27}
\]

Because \(\Phi\) is invertible, its differential has no physical kernel. Therefore

\[
\frac{\delta S_{\rm TPV}}
{\delta\mathcal C^A}=0
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
E^{\mu\nu}=0.
\tag{28}
\]

Hence the TPV equations of motion are precisely

\[
\boxed{
G_{\mu\nu}
+
\Lambda g_{\mu\nu}
=
\kappa T_{\mu\nu}.
}
\tag{29}
\]

### Equivalence theorem

Under fixed dimension, Lorentzian signature, orientation and time orientation, and with matter universally coupled to \(g[\mathcal C]\),

\[
\boxed{
\mathrm{Sol}(\mathrm{TPV})
/
\mathrm{Diff}(M)
\;\cong\;
\mathrm{Sol}(\mathrm{GR})
/
\mathrm{Diff}(M).
}
\tag{30}
\]

Every GR spacetime defines one TPV spacetime, every admissible TPV spacetime reconstructs one GR spacetime, and the correspondence respects diffeomorphism equivalence.

Equation (30) is the precise meaning of the claim that baseline TPV is an interpretation of general relativity rather than a modification of it.

