## 5. Diffeomorphism covariance

For a diffeomorphism \(\varphi:M\rightarrow M\),

\[
g\rightarrow\varphi^\ast g.
\tag{31}
\]

Because both the Hodge operator and volume form are natural geometric constructions,

\[
\mathcal C[\varphi^\ast g]
=
\varphi^\ast\mathcal C[g].
\tag{32}
\]

Thus the constitutive reformulation does not select a preferred coordinate system.

Nor does TPV possess a preferred physical rest frame. An observer with four-velocity \(u^\mu\) may decompose the covariant constitutive relation into electric, magnetic and magnetoelectric response tensors, but these are observer-dependent projections of the same covariant object.

This distinction is important. Familiar relations of the Plebanski type,

\[
D^i
=
\varepsilon^{ij}E_j
+
\gamma^{ij}H_j,
\qquad
B^i
=
\mu^{ij}H_j
-
\gamma^{ij}E_j,
\tag{33}
\]

with schematic identifications such as

\[
\varepsilon^{ij}
=
\mu^{ij}
\propto
-\frac{\sqrt{-g}}{g_{00}}g^{ij},
\qquad
\gamma_i
\propto
\frac{g_{0i}}{g_{00}},
\tag{34}
\]

are useful coordinate representations, but they are not the fundamental TPV variables. Plebanski's original construction and later transformation-optics interpretations establish the mathematical correspondence, while covariant observer analyses show why an ordinary flat-background material interpretation should not be taken literally.

TPV therefore uses Eq. (11), not a coordinate-specific \(\epsilon\)-\(\mu\) tensor, as its fundamental constitutive definition.

