## 6. Matter coupling and conservation

Matter couples through

\[
S_m[g[\mathcal C],\Psi].
\tag{35}
\]

There is only one physical metric. All nongravitational sectors therefore use the same proper times, spatial distances and null cones.

The stress-energy tensor is defined conventionally,

\[
T_{\mu\nu}
=
-\frac{2}{\sqrt{-g}}
\frac{\delta S_m}
{\delta g^{\mu\nu}}.
\tag{36}
\]

The contracted Bianchi identity,

\[
\nabla^\mu G_{\mu\nu}=0,
\tag{37}
\]

together with Eq. (29), gives

\[
\boxed{
\nabla^\mu T_{\mu\nu}=0.
}
\tag{38}
\]

No additional “vacuum-medium” energy exchange appears because the TPV constitutive state is not an independent matter sector.

A description such as “matter polarizes the vacuum” is therefore shorthand for

\[
T_{\mu\nu}
\longrightarrow
g_{\mu\nu}
\longleftrightarrow
\mathcal C[g].
\tag{39}
\]

Matter changes spacetime geometry according to Einstein's equation; the same change can equivalently be described as a change of the spacetime constitutive state.

