## 7. First-order and metric-affine representation

The same equivalence may be displayed in first-order form. Consider the Einstein-Palatini action

\[
S_{\rm EP}
=
\frac{1}{2\kappa}
\int d^4x\sqrt{-g}
\left[
g^{\mu\nu}R_{(\mu\nu)}(\Gamma)
-2\Lambda
\right]
+
S_m[g,\Psi],
\tag{40}
\]

where \(g_{\mu\nu}\) and \(\Gamma^\lambda{}_{\mu\nu}\) are initially independent and matter does not couple independently to the affine connection.

The connection equation is algebraic in the appropriate geometric sense and fixes

\[
\Gamma^\lambda{}_{\mu\nu}
=
\left\{
\begin{matrix}
\lambda\\
\mu\nu
\end{matrix}
\right\}_g
+
\delta^\lambda{}_\nu A_\mu ,
\tag{41}
\]

up to a projective gauge field \(A_\mu\). The projective contribution drops from the symmetric Ricci tensor entering Eq. (40), and the metric field equation becomes the ordinary Einstein equation. Hamiltonian analyses of the Einstein-Palatini formulation likewise find no additional physical gravitational degrees of freedom associated with the connection.

Thus the connection may be interpreted as part of the geometric/constitutive representation without becoming an independent propagating substance.

This observation also clarifies the relation between baseline TPV and broader Ricci-based metric-affine theories. Projectively invariant Ricci-based theories can often be mapped to an Einstein-frame description, but nontrivial deformation matrices generally generate altered matter interactions. They are therefore not automatically identical to GR.

For **exactly equivalent TPV**, the gravitational theory is instead Eq. (23) or its Einstein-Palatini equivalent. Matter-dependent metric-affine deformations belong to possible *active extensions* and must not be invoked in the proof of exact GR equivalence.

