## 8. Hamiltonian constraints and degrees of freedom

Writing the metric in ADM form,

\[
ds^2
=
-N^2dt^2
+
\gamma_{ij}
(dx^i+N^idt)
(dx^j+N^jdt),
\tag{42}
\]

the Einstein-Hilbert action becomes, up to boundary terms,

\[
S_g
=
\frac{1}{2\kappa}
\int dt\,d^3x\,
N\sqrt{\gamma}
\left[
{}^{(3)}R
+
K_{ij}K^{ij}
-
K^2
-
2\Lambda
\right].
\tag{43}
\]

The lapse \(N\) and shift \(N^i\) possess no independent kinetic terms. Their variations impose the Hamiltonian and momentum constraints,

\[
\mathcal H_\perp=0,
\qquad
\mathcal H_i=0.
\tag{44}
\]

For the six components of \(\gamma_{ij}\) and six conjugate momenta, four first-class constraints and four associated gauge directions remove eight phase-space dimensions:

\[
12-2(4)=4.
\tag{45}
\]

Thus the gravitational phase space contains

\[
\frac{4}{2}=2
\tag{46}
\]

local configuration-space degrees of freedom.

These are the two tensor polarizations of general relativity.

The TPV constitutive transformation is invertible and does not alter the rank of the Legendre map or the constraint structure. Therefore

\[
\boxed{
N_{\rm DOF}^{\rm TPV}
=
N_{\rm DOF}^{\rm GR}
=
2.
}
\tag{47}
\]

Standard Hamiltonian treatments of GR explicitly recover this two-degree-of-freedom structure. Recent work has also shown directly how ADM metric variables can be translated into constitutive/optical variables, including correspondence between spatial metric, shift, material response and the two TT gravitational-wave degrees of freedom.

