## 9. Linear gravitational waves

Let

\[
g_{\mu\nu}
=
\eta_{\mu\nu}
+
h_{\mu\nu}.
\tag{48}
\]

In vacuum, after imposing the usual transverse-traceless conditions,

\[
h_{0\mu}^{\rm TT}=0,
\qquad
\partial^ih_{ij}^{\rm TT}=0,
\qquad
h^{i}{}_{i}^{\rm TT}=0,
\tag{49}
\]

the field equation is

\[
\Box h_{ij}^{\rm TT}=0.
\tag{50}
\]

There are two independent polarizations.

The corresponding first-order constitutive perturbation follows from Eq. (6):

\[
\begin{aligned}
\delta\chi^{\mu\nu\rho\sigma}
={}&
\frac{h}{2}
\left(
\eta^{\mu\rho}\eta^{\nu\sigma}
-
\eta^{\mu\sigma}\eta^{\nu\rho}
\right)
\\
&
-
h^{\mu\rho}\eta^{\nu\sigma}
-\eta^{\mu\rho}h^{\nu\sigma}
+h^{\mu\sigma}\eta^{\nu\rho}
+\eta^{\mu\sigma}h^{\nu\rho}.
\end{aligned}
\tag{51}
\]

For a TT perturbation,

\[
h=0.
\tag{52}
\]

Thus the two physical components of \(h_{ij}^{\rm TT}\) map directly to two tensorial perturbations of the constitutive state.

A TPV gravitational wave is therefore not a new wave travelling through a separate substrate. It is the ordinary GR gravitational wave expressed as a propagating tensor variation of vacuum-spacetime polarization.

No third polarization is generated.

No scalar polarization is generated.

No additional dispersion relation is generated.

In baseline TPV,

\[
\boxed{
\omega^2=c^2k^2,
}
\tag{53}
\]

exactly as in GR.

