## 10. Curvature as constitutive geometry

The phrase “curvature is a change in polarization” requires refinement.

A metric can vary with coordinates even in flat spacetime, so ordinary coordinate gradients of constitutive components are not themselves curvature.

Instead, because

\[
g=g[\mathcal C],
\tag{54}
\]

the Riemann tensor may be written formally as

\[
R^\rho{}_{\sigma\mu\nu}
=
R^\rho{}_{\sigma\mu\nu}
[\mathcal C,\partial\mathcal C,\partial^2\mathcal C].
\tag{55}
\]

Curvature is the invariant obstruction to transforming the constitutive spacetime state into the constant Minkowski state throughout an extended region.

At any point \(p\), local inertial coordinates may be chosen so that

\[
g_{\mu\nu}(p)=\eta_{\mu\nu},
\qquad
\partial_\alpha g_{\mu\nu}(p)=0.
\tag{56}
\]

Correspondingly,

\[
\mathcal C(p)
=
\mathcal C_{\rm Minkowski},
\tag{57}
\]

to local inertial order.

What remains physically invariant is tidal structure,

\[
R^\rho{}_{\sigma\mu\nu}(p)\neq0.
\tag{58}
\]

TPV therefore interprets curvature as the covariant spatial-temporal structure of the constitutive state, not as an absolute local polarization detectable independently of an observer.

This reproduces rather than replaces the equivalence principle.

