## 12. Exact observational equivalence

Let \(\mathcal O[g,\Psi]\) be any diffeomorphism-invariant observable of GR.

Define the corresponding TPV observable by

\[
\mathcal O_{\rm TPV}[\mathcal C,\Psi]
=
\mathcal O[g[\mathcal C],\Psi].
\tag{62}
\]

For corresponding solutions,

\[
\boxed{
\mathcal O_{\rm TPV}
=
\mathcal O_{\rm GR}.
}
\tag{63}
\]

This includes, without limitation,

- gravitational redshift;
- light deflection;
- Shapiro delay;
- perihelion precession;
- frame dragging;
- binary-pulsar dynamics;
- gravitational-wave generation and propagation;
- black-hole geometries;
- cosmological expansion;
- neutron-star structure;
- tidal Love numbers;
- quasinormal spectra; and
- strong-field causal structure.

No experiment can distinguish baseline TPV from GR because the two formulations possess the same solution space and observables.

This is a feature rather than a deficiency for an interpretive theory.

